\f0\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 For your archive, I would make 
\f1\b your own internal bibliographic layer
\f0\b0 , then export/crosswalk to CSL-JSON, Zotero, Dublin Core, schema.org, etc. None of the existing standards alone is right for your use case because you need both 
\f1\b citation metadata
\f0\b0  and 
\f1\b forensic archive provenance
\f0\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Core Recommendation
\f0\b0 \
Use:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 CSL-JSON
\f0\b0  as the main citation/export model.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Zotero-style fields
\f0\b0  as the practical researcher-facing editing model.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Dublin Core
\f0\b0  as a simple public/archive interoperability layer.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 schema.org JSON-LD
\f0\b0  for public Wikia pages.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Crossref/DataCite/OpenAlex/OpenLibrary/OCLC
\f0\b0  as enrichment sources, never as unquestioned truth.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Your own 
\f2\fs26 bibliographic.json
\f1\fs24  + 
\f2\fs26 references.json
\f0\b0\fs24  as the canonical archive records.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 CSL-JSON is good for generating citations because each item has a required 
\f3\fs26 id
\f0\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 type
\f0\fs24 , and citation variables such as author, title, issued date, DOI, URL, ISBN, publisher, and container metadata ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://citeproc-js.readthedocs.io/en/latest/csl-json/markup.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 CSL-JSON / citeproc-js docs}}). Zotero is useful because its field model maps closely to how humanities researchers actually describe books, reports, theses, legal items, archives, URLs, access dates, publishers, places, ISBNs, DOIs, archives, and call numbers ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.zotero.org/support/kb/item_types_and_fields"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Zotero item fields}}).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

